{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217812","model_call":18,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":10099,"output_tokens":452,"total_tokens":10551,"cached_tokens":8960,"reasoning_tokens":238},"output_characters":951,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1370231623#4_2504753484","relevance":2,"reason":"This canthal-tilt cosmetic surgery page gives direct partial evidence about why positive tilt may diminish: aging is multifactorial, and smoking/sun damage reduce eyelid tissue elasticity and supporting structures. It supports age/soft-tissue laxity as a reason some people lose positive canthal tilt, but it is not a complete explanation of why some people naturally have it.","learned":"Canthal tilt can diminish with aging and tissue laxity; smoking and sun damage may contribute by reducing elasticity in lower eyelid skin and supporting structures."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"positive canthal tilt\" (+ genetics genetic inherited hereditary facial anatomy))","reason":"Search directly for documents linking natural positive canthal tilt to inherited/genetic facial and eyelid anatomy, which would best answer why some people have it."}}}
