{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217812","model_call":20,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":11332,"output_tokens":409,"total_tokens":11741,"cached_tokens":10496,"reasoning_tokens":142},"output_characters":1108,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_59_603346943#2_1468435431","relevance":2,"reason":"The full document is dedicated to canthal tilt and gives useful supporting evidence: positive canthal tilt is a 5–8 degree upward angle between the medial and lateral canthi, is associated with youth/feminine appearance, and aging loosens eyelid tendons so the canthus becomes more negative. However, it still does not fully explain why some people naturally have positive tilt beyond sex/youth and tendon laxity.","learned":"Positive canthal tilt depends on the angle between the canthi; aging can make tilt more negative as eyelid tendons loosen, so youthful/tighter eyelid support helps maintain a positive tilt."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"palpebral fissure slant\" \"palpebral fissure inclination\" \"canthal tilt\") (+ \"lateral canthus\" \"medial canthus\") (+ genetics ethnic sex age anatomy))","reason":"Search for more clinical or anthropometric documents that explain natural positive tilt as variation in palpebral fissure slant related to anatomy, genetics, ethnicity, sex, or age."}}}
