{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217812","model_call":27,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"lateral canthal angle\" \"medial canthal angle\" (+ higher asians europeans women men))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"lateral canthal angle\" \"medial canthal angle\" (+ higher asians europeans women men))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_299850012#0_636416191","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2550","rank":1,"snippet":"Septum Orbital Fat Pads \" , \"segment\": \"Eyelid Anatomy | Plastic Surgery Key Eyelid Anatomy 15 Eyelid Anatomy Catherine Y. Liu, Swapna Vemuri, and Jeremiah P. Tao Surface Anatomy The eyelids provide globe protection, contribute to tear production, and distribute tears. The adjacent forehead and midface influence correct eyelid positioning. Understanding these relationships is essential in eyelid surgery. The upper and lower eyelids, along with the upper and lower puncta, oppose the globe. The upper lid naturally rests 1 to 2 mm below the superior limbus and peaks 1 mm medial to the center of the pupil. The lower lid rests at the inferior limbus and peaks 1 mm lateral to the center of the pupil. Horizontal and vertical interpalpebral fissures are about 30 mm and 10 mm, respectively. The <cover>lateral canthal angle is about 2 mm higher than the medial canthal angle. </cover>The medial canthal angle is slightly rounded compared with the sharply peaked lateral canthal angle ( Fig. 15.1 ).\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Eyelid Anatomy | Plastic Surgery Key\" , \"url\": \"https://plasticsurgerykey.com/eyelid-anatomy/\" }"},"time":82},"model_calls":27,"document_judgments":18,"relevance_3":0,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
