{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217812","model_call":30,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":16057,"output_tokens":386,"total_tokens":16443,"cached_tokens":15104,"reasoning_tokens":160},"output_characters":988,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_846829933#4_1744984227","relevance":2,"reason":"The page is dedicated to canthal tilt and gives partial causal evidence: positive tilt is associated with young people, while negative tilt is associated with aging and excessive upper/lower eyelid skin. It indirectly explains that people may have positive tilt when youthful eyelid support has not drooped, but it is not a full direct explanation of why some naturally have it.","learned":"Positive canthal tilt is associated with youth; negative canthal tilt is associated with aging and excess eyelid skin causing a drooped/tired appearance."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"canthal tilt\" (+ genetics ethnicity anatomy aging) (+ \"lateral canthus\" \"orbital rim\" \"lateral canthal tendon\"))","reason":"Search for documents combining canthal tilt with specific anatomical structures and demographic causes, likely to explain why the lateral canthus sits higher in some people."}}}
