{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217812","model_call":48,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":27497,"output_tokens":524,"total_tokens":28021,"cached_tokens":26368,"reasoning_tokens":309},"output_characters":891,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_524764763#3_934428153","relevance":2,"reason":"This is another segment from the dedicated palpebral-fissure clinical reference. It confirms that upslanting/upslanted palpebral fissures are a recognized finding and gives terminology/ontology support, but this passage does not show the causes list or fully explain why some people have the trait.","learned":"Upslanting palpebral fissure is a recognized clinical finding synonymous with upslanted palpebral fissure, matching the upward/positive canthal-tilt concept."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"lateral canthus\" \"medial canthus\" (+ higher lower) (+ ethnic sex women men anthropometric normal))","reason":"Look for anatomical or anthropometric evidence explaining normal positive canthal tilt as population/sex variation in the relative height of the lateral and medial canthi."}}}
