{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-217812","model_call":55,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"upward slanting palpebral fissures\" (+ causes associated syndrome genetic chromosomal))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"upward slanting palpebral fissures\" (+ causes associated syndrome genetic chromosomal))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1404150389#10_2212327399","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2571","rank":1,"snippet":"\" , \"segment\": \"Nystagmus may also account for a substantial extent of visual acuity loss. Other common causes of decreased visual acuity in Down syndrome patients include cataracts and high refractive errors. Accommodation Poor accommodative ability has been found in Down syndrome children. Using dynamic retinoscopy, studies have found that 32% to 100% of Down syndrome children have an abnormally low ability to accommodate, measured by accommodative response. Inaccurate accommodation has been found to be associated with hypermetropia and strabismus. Eyelids and Midface The most common findings are <cover>upward slanting palpebral fissures, with a prevalence ranging from 63% to 82% in Down syndrome </cover>children, and large epicanthal folds, ranging from 31% to 97%. Blepharitis is also a common finding in Down syndrome patients. The prevalence of blepharitis in children with Down syndrome ranges from 3% to 34.5%. The wide range in prevalence may be due to differing criteria for diagnosing blepharitis. The high rate of blepharitis has been speculated to be due to impaired immune or due to skin abnormalities in Down syndrome individuals.\" , \"start_char\": 7014.000000 , \"title\": \"Trisomy 21/Down Syndrome - "},"time":87},"model_calls":55,"document_judgments":44,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
